"Permission rules the plugin REQUIRES for the §3.7 single-permission contract to function. Plugin-level settings.json's `permissions` block is silently ignored by Claude Code, so these rules can't ship as plugin defaults. /lua-doctor offers to merge this into the user's project `.claude/settings.json` on first run; the user accepts once and the rules persist for the project. Every command shape here is verified against lua-cli 3.33.0 (its src/cli/command-definitions.ts and src/utils/aliases.ts); the workflows policy / clear-gate / recompose rules against lua-cli 3.36.0. HOW THE PRODUCTION GATE WORKS (verified live against Claude Code on 2026-09-12 and documented at code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions: 'A deny or ask rule matches past any leading assignment' and 'Hook decisions don't bypass permission rules'): the bare production verbs (`lua deploy …`, `lua version promote …`, …) are deliberately NOT listed in `deny` or `ask` — such a rule also matches the user-confirmed form `LUA_DEPLOY_CONFIRMED=1 lua deploy …` and would make every deploy impossible. The gate for the bare forms is hooks/confirm-deploy.mjs (a PreToolUse exit-2 block wins over any allow rule, including a broad `Bash(lua *)` in your own settings); the `allow` entries below are the literal prefixed forms the deploy flow emits after your single confirmation, so they run without a second prompt. A bare verb that somehow reaches the permission layer falls to Claude Code's default (a prompt; a denial in `-p` mode). The alternative binaries `heylua` and `lua-ai` are the same program and are denied wholesale — the plugin only ever emits `lua`. test/lib/permissions-mirror.test.mjs fails if any deny/ask rule would shadow a confirmed form or any allow rule admits a bare one. Precedence is deny → ask → allow. The `ask` tier IS the single confirmation for workflow run-control verbs (start/approve/signal/cancel…): /lua-workflow relies on this prompt and does not AskUserQuestion on top. lua-cli 3.36.0 adds `lua workflows policy models|autonomy get|set` (an ORGANIZATION-wide policy — `get` is read-only and allowed, `set` asks: `policy autonomy set --enabled on` decides which workflow starts skip the consent card), `clear-gate` (alias `ungate`; clears a `model_policy` park — run control, asks) and `recompose` (alias `recompile`; publishes a new version of a chat-composed definition — asks). lua-cli 3.38.0 adds `lua drains` (log drains — an ORGANIZATION resource, not a deploy): the read verbs `list`, `status`, `deliveries` and the one-record `test` are allowed, and the seven that change the org's configuration — `create`, `update`, `delete`, `verify`, `pause`, `resume`, `rotate-secret` — sit in `ask`, which IS /lua-drains's single confirmation. They are NOT in lib/tokenizer.mjs: nothing a drain verb does changes what runs in production, the `LUA_DEPLOY_CONFIRMED=1` prefix means ‘the user confirmed a DEPLOY’ and confirm-deploy would send the user to /lua-deploy for a log-shipping change. The per-verb spelling matters — a blanket `Bash(lua drains *)` in `ask` would prompt for a read, and one in `allow` would admit `rotate-secret`. Secrets never reach a command line: a header value is prompted or read from an env var (`--header-from-env NAME=ENV_VAR`) and the signing secret is minted server-side and printed once, so there is no credential-printing rule to deny here. /lua-env (`lua env *` — deliberately ask even for `--list`, since the CLI prints masked values) and /lua-integrations (connect|update|disconnect|convert, webhooks create|pause|resume|delete, mcp activate|deactivate) rely on the same prompt. MCP: Claude Code names a plugin's MCP tools mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__<tool> (verified live 2026-09-12), so the read-only lua-platform server is allowed as mcp__plugin_lua-agent-builder_lua-platform (all five tools are GET-only) plus the two docs search tools; submit_feedback is deliberately not pre-approved. The bare mcp__lua-platform / mcp__lua-docs spellings cover a server the user registered with `claude mcp add`. 1.7.0 (lua-cli 3.44.0 / 3.45.0): `lua devices list|status` stay allowed; every `lua devices` verb that changes a device, sends a real command or fires a live handler (`enable|disable` and their `on|off|activate|deactivate` aliases, `test` (`run|exec`), `test-trigger`, `remove` (`rm|del`, plus the generic remove/delete rows), and the 3.45.0 `credential` (`credentials|key`)) sits in `ask` — that prompt IS /lua-devices's single confirmation. `lua push device …` and `lua push device-trigger …` (every push-type alias) are in `ask` too, which wins over the generic `lua push * --ci --force*` allow: a pushed defineDevice is live on the agent's next turn without any publish, so that push is a production change (devices are not part of agent versions). None of these is a tokenizer verb — `LUA_DEPLOY_CONFIRMED=1` means ‘the user confirmed a deploy’ and /lua-deploy does not ship devices. `lua devices credential` additionally needs `--out <file>`: hooks/block-device-secret.mjs blocks it otherwise, because without it the secret is printed into the conversation. 1.8.0 (web apps, lua-cli 3.42.0+): `lua apps new <name>` (a local scaffold that edits src/index.ts and tsconfig.json — nothing reaches the server) and the page-project npm commands are deliberately NOT here: a glob `*` matches spaces, so `Bash(npm --prefix src/apps/*/web install)` also admitted an extra package, a second `--prefix` and `../` paths. a trailing `lua apps new *` likewise admitted any text after the name. hooks/approve-web-app.mjs approves exactly `lua apps new <name>`, `npm --prefix src/apps/<name>/web install --ignore-scripts [@lua-ai-global/app-client]`, `run typecheck` and `run build` with anchored regexes instead; anything else gets Claude Code\u2019s normal prompt. `lua push webapp` rides the generic `lua push * --ci --force*` allow — a web-app version is staged only and goes live with an agent version, whose promote is the gated verb. `lua test --ci webapp …` rides `lua test --ci*` like every test type, although a non-GET route writes live Data: the confirmation for those is /lua-test\u2019s own AskUserQuestion, because a permission glob cannot tell the HTTP method inside the quoted `--route` reliably. `lua apps dev` is deliberately in no list (a long-running server whose routes write live Data — the plugin never runs it, so Claude Code\u2019s default prompt applies). lua-cli 3.44.0 also added `lua skills production deploy` (a non-interactive skill deploy — gated in lib/tokenizer.mjs, prefixed form allowed above), `lua logs export` (an org-wide export that can include message bodies; `--ci` binds anywhere, so `lua logs --ci export` would otherwise ride the `lua logs --ci*` allow rule — both spellings ask) and `lua drains confirm|replay` (ask). 1.9.0: `Bash(node *lua-agent-builder*/lib/qa/cli.mjs *)` runs the /lua-qa full-suite helpers; they spawn lua internally with a scrubbed env and a coded argv allowlist (lib/qa/safety.mjs, checked again by the deploy classifier). The glob alone would also admit any other file named lib/qa/cli.mjs under a path containing `lua-agent-builder` (one an agent wrote into /tmp or the project), so hooks/guard-qa-helper.mjs resolves the script's real path and blocks every call that is not this plugin's own helper; with that hook the rule admits only the genuine helper, which has no production verb. `Bash(node *lib/qa/cli.mjs*--production-consent-text*)` sits in `ask` and wins over that allow: stamping the production (or staged-without-test-session) consent gate is the one helper call Claude Code itself confirms with the user, so no agent can mint production consent without a prompt. That prompt is deliberate, on top of /lua-qa's consent question. 1.10.0 (lua-cli 3.48.0+, `--model` 3.49.2+): `lua decisions` is AGENT configuration, not a deploy. `list`, `versions` and `run` are allowed (`run` is one billed decision call that changes nothing), and `publish` (makes a version current for every caller, the rollback) and `delete` (deactivates; `Decisions.run` then throws decision_not_found) sit in `ask`, which IS /lua-decisions's single confirmation. Per-verb spelling again: a blanket `Bash(lua decisions *)` in `allow` would admit `delete`. `lua push decision` rides the generic `lua push * --ci --force*` allow because a pushed decision is staged only — it goes live with `lua version promote` (gated, /lua-deploy) or with `lua decisions publish` (ask). Not in lib/tokenizer.mjs: `LUA_DEPLOY_CONFIRMED=1` means a deploy, and publishing a decision version is agent config the ask prompt already covers. 1.14.0: the `ui` MCP server (https://ui.lua.dev/mcp, the public Storybook catalog of @lua-ai-global/ui) is allowed tool by tool, in both spellings: list-all-documentation, get-documentation and get-documentation-for-story, which only read component docs. Never as a whole server (`mcp__plugin_lua-agent-builder_ui` / `mcp__ui`): a remote server can add tools at any time, and a whole-server rule would pre-approve them unseen — scripts/lint-permissions.mjs and the mirror test forbid a whole-server rule for every remote (http/sse) server in .mcp.json. The key is `ui`, not `lua-ui`, so every composed name stays within 64 characters (mcp__plugin_lua-agent-builder_lua-ui__get-documentation-for-story was 65). 1.15.0 (lua-cli 3.49.0+; `lua browser run --login` 3.57.0): the cloud browser. `lua browser read <url>` and `lua browsers list` are allowed — a read opens one cloud session, prints the page, closes it and changes nothing at the other end (the same shape as the allowed `lua decisions run`: billed, no state change). `lua browser run` sits in `ask`, and that prompt IS /lua-browser's single confirmation: a run clicks, types and can submit (Buy, Pay, Place order, Sign in, Send) on a real third-party website, spends up to `--max-cost` (default 1 USD, at most 10) of model budget, and with `--login` acts as the person on that site — effects no Lua verb undoes. `lua browsers delete` is in `ask` too (per-verb, like `lua decisions delete`, although the generic `lua * delete*` row would also catch it), with its `rm`/`del`/`remove` aliases. `lua push browser` / `lua push browsers` sit in `ask` ABOVE the generic `lua push * --ci --force*` allow, for the same reason as `lua push device`: a browser is not versioned, the push upserts the profile by name, so for a browser the agent already lists in `browsers` it rewrites the live policy (allowedDomains, confirmActions, allowScripts, maxDownloadMb) for the next session, with no agent version to gate or roll it back. None of them is a lib/tokenizer.mjs verb: nothing here changes what runs in production, and `LUA_DEPLOY_CONFIRMED=1` means 'the user confirmed a DEPLOY'. Per-verb spelling matters as always — a blanket `Bash(lua browser *)` in `allow` would admit `run`, and one in `ask` would prompt for a read."
